    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d65">

                <pb facs="0121" n="39" sameAs="frus:frus1945Malta/pg39"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to President Roosevelt
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d65-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-24">January 24,
                        1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Prime Minister to President Roosevelt Personal and Top Secret number 894.</p>
                <p>It would be a great pity if Eisenhower and Alexander only come to <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">Cricket</hi> and if we do not have them with us at
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Magneto</hi>.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d65-f2"/>
                    This will really make it impossible for the Heads of Government to enter fully
                    into the military problems. I hope therefore they may be instructed as
                    originally proposed to come to <hi rendition="#small-caps">Magneto</hi> as well
                    as <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cricket</hi> and if they have to be absent from
                    either, it should be <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cricket</hi>.</p>
                <p>The above of course is subject to battle exigencies.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d65-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d65-f1-content">
                <p>Sent by the United States Military Attaché, London, via Army channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d65-f2" n="2" xml:id="d65-f2-content">
                <p>Alexander was present at both Malta and Yalta, but Eisenhower was not. The
                    latter’s chief of staff, Lieutenant General Walter Bedell Smith, was, however,
                    in attendance at Malta.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
